    'text' => 'Outline 1. Where Does the Money Come From? Federal grants (FEMA, HUD) State funding (North Carolina disaster relief) Private foundations (Cisco, Land of Sky, etc.) Partner organizations (Habitat, CHC, etc.) Individual donations 2. The $50,000 Per Home Cap Each eligible household is capped at $50,000 for disaster-related repairs This is per disaster, per home (if another disaster hits, new cap applies) Funds must be spent on eligible repairs only The cap applies to total cost across all funding sources 3. How We Track Funding Per Case Approved Amount: What we\'ve committed to spend Incurred Costs: What we\'ve actually spent (invoices, materials, labor) Reimbursed Amount: What has been paid out Remaining Balance: What\'s left to spend On Hold: Money set aside for pending items 4. The Three Key Financial Dashboards Dashboard Purpose User Past Week Monitor recent activity All staff All Time Track historical performance All staff Organization Specific Per-org metrics Admin, Bursar 5. The Metrics We Track Past Week: Total Applications Assessments Assigned &amp; Completed Average Triage Score Total Individuals Served (from household size) Income, County, Issue Type Breakdowns All Time: Total Jobs Jobs In Progress / Completed / Withdrawn Average Job Cost Income &amp; County Breakdowns Highest &amp; Lowest Job Costs Organization Specific: Total Jobs per org Jobs In Progress / Completed / Suggested Assessments Assigned / Completed / Pending Cost Extremes and Averages 6. Reimbursement Workflow Subcontractor submits invoice → enters queue Bursar reviews (checks against scope, budget) Approve or reject with reason If approved → payment issued Track aging: goal &lt; 30 days 7. Preventing Double-Dipping Case locking prevents two orgs from claiming same case Duplicate address detection Overlapping invoice alerts Every payment has audit trail',
