    'excerpt' => 'Application Review &amp; Case Management Who this is for: Caseworkers, Admins, and Organizational Administrators who review incoming applications and turn them into active cases. W',
    'text' => 'Application Review &amp; Case Management Who this is for: Caseworkers, Admins, and Organizational Administrators who review incoming applications and turn them into active cases. Why This Matters This is where applications become cases. It\'s the bridge between "someone asked for help" and "we\'re actually helping them." Mess this up and you get bottlenecks, dropped applicants, and the kind of frustration that makes partners pull out of the coalition. Get it right and the whole system flows smoothly. If you didn\'t review it, it doesn\'t exist. Every application needs eyes on it before it becomes a case. Step 1: Check the Pending Applications Queue Your dashboard should show you a list of unclaimed applications. These are submissions from applicants that haven\'t been picked up by any organization yet. What to look for: Submission date — older applications first (FIFO, unless urgency dictates otherwise) Urgency flags — "Unable to stay in home," "No potable water," "Open to elements" — these jump the queue Completeness — does the application have basic contact info, address, and household details? Tip: An incomplete application is still worth reviewing. Sometimes missing info just means the applicant didn\'t know what to enter. You can fill gaps during first contact. Step 2: Review Applicant Information Open the application and check for: Item Why It Matters Home address / Placecode Confirms they\'re in your service area Contact information Phone, email — at least one must be valid Home ownership Must own the property to qualify for most programs Household size &amp; income Determines eligibility tier Repair needs listed Tells you what kind of work they need Damage cause Hurricane Helene-related? Different funding paths apply Eligibility flags Green = likely eligible, Yellow = needs review, Red = potential disqualifier Remember our philosophy: Assume eligibility until proven otherwise. The system flags issues for human review — it never auto-denies. A yellow or red flag means "look closer," not "reject." Step 3: Eligibility Check The system runs automatic eligibility checks based on: Geography — is the property in your service area? Income — does household income fall within program guidelines? Home ownership — does the applicant own the property? Damage cause — is the damage related to a covered event? What the flags mean: Flag Color Meaning Action 🟢 Green Meets standard criteria Proceed normally 🟡 Yellow Something needs review Look at the flagged item, decide if it\'s a real issue 🔴 Red Potential disqualifier Review carefully — this needs a human decision with documented reasoning Important: A red flag does NOT mean auto-denial. It means a human needs to review the situation and check boxes explaining the decision. We don\'t let algorithms turn people away. Step 4: Claim the Application (Create a Case) Once you\'ve reviewed the application and decided your organization can help: Click "Claim" on the application record Select the repair needs your organization will handle (you might not handle all of them) Add a claim note — something brief like "Roof assessment scheduled for 9/15" Confirm What happens next: A Case is created, linked to the original Application The 90-day progress clock starts — you now have 90 days to show progress, or the claim expires and another org can pick it up The application status updates to show it\'s been claimed Other organizations can see that these repair needs are taken (but can still claim unclaimed needs) The 90-day rule exists for a reason. It prevents applications from sitting in limbo. Any progress event — a task, a communication, a document upload — resets the clock. So keep the case moving. Step 5: Set Initial Case Status and Assign Staff After claiming: Set the case status — typically starts at "New" or "Intake Review" Assign a caseworker — the primary point of contact for this household Log the first progress event — "Case claimed by [Org], assigned to [Caseworker]" Best practice: Make first contact within 48 hours of claiming. Applicants who submit and then hear nothing get anxious, and anxious applicants call other organizations, which creates duplicate work and confusion. Step 6: When to Accept vs. Pass on an Application Accept when: The repair need matches your organization\'s capabilities You have capacity to handle it in a reasonable timeframe The applicant is in your service area Pass (don\'t claim) when: The repair need is outside your scope (e.g., you do roofs, they need electrical) You\'re at capacity and can\'t start work within 90 days The applicant is outside your geographic service area If you pass, log why. A quick note like "Electrical repair outside our scope — referred to [Other Org]" helps the next organization that looks at this application. Common Scenarios Scenario 1: Two Organizations, One Application An application has roof damage AND plumbing damage. Habitat claims the roof. CHC claims the plumbing. Two cases, one application. This is normal and expected. The Application layer is shared; each Case has its own timeline and progress. Scenario 2: Application Has Been Claimed but Nothing Is Happening Check the 90-day clock. If no progress events have been logged, the claim may be about to expire. You can reach out to the claiming organization or wait for the claim to expire and then claim it yourself. Scenario 3: Applicant Submitted Twice The system deduplicates submissions into a single Application. If the same household submits multiple times (forgot to upload docs, changed their mind, etc.), all submissions roll into one Application record. Check the submission history to see the full picture. Quick Reference Action Where Who View pending applications Case Search / Dashboard Admin, Caseworker Review eligibility flags Application detail view Admin, Caseworker Claim repair needs Application detail → "Claim" button Admin, Caseworker Assign caseworker Case detail → Staff assignment Admin Set case status Case detail → Status dropdown Admin, Caseworker Log progress event Any case action auto-logs; manual via "Log Event" Everyone Remember: Every application is a person who needs help. The faster you review, the faster they get it.',
  ),
  27 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'completing-repair-case',
    'title' => 'Completing a Case & Closing Out SOP',
    'category' => 'Standard Operating Procedures',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'case-closure',
      1 => 'inspection',
      2 => 'sign-off',
      3 => 'invoicing',
      4 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Completing a Case &amp; Closing Out Who this is for: Project Managers doing final inspections, Caseworkers coordinating sign-off, and Bursars reconciling finances. Why This Is Fina',
    'text' => 'Completing a Case &amp; Closing Out Who this is for: Project Managers doing final inspections, Caseworkers coordinating sign-off, and Bursars reconciling finances. Why This Is Final Proper closure ensures legal protection, grant compliance, and happy applicants. A case that isn\'t closed properly can come back to haunt you — literally and financially. An open case with completed work is a liability. Funders ask questions. Applicants get confused. Data gets messy. Close it right. Step 1: Final Inspection Checklist Before closing a case, the Project Manager should verify: Work Completion [ ] All tasks in the project are marked Complete [ ] Each task has photos — before and after, clearly labeled [ ] Hours are logged — volunteer and paid hours both [ ] Materials are recorded — what was used, what cost [ ] Quality checks passed — no rework needed Property Condition [ ] Worksite is clean — no debris, tools, or materials left behind [ ] All repairs match scope — nothing missed, nothing extra (unless change-ordered) [ ] No new damage — the repair didn\'t create new problems [ ] Utilities are functional — water, electricity, HVAC all working Documentation [ ] After photos uploaded — same angles as before photos [ ] Assessment referenced — the original assessment is linked to the completed work [ ] Progress events logged — the timeline tells the full story [ ] Change orders documented — any scope changes have approval records Step 2: Applicant Sign-Off The applicant should confirm they\'re satisfied with the work: Walk through the repairs with the applicant Explain what was done — in plain language, not construction jargon Ask for sign-off — a verbal confirmation at minimum, a signature if your program requires it Address any concerns — if the applicant isn\'t satisfied, note the issue and assign a corrective task What if the applicant isn\'t happy? Don\'t close the case. Assign a follow-up task, fix the issue, and do another walkthrough. A closed case with an unhappy applicant is a complaint waiting to happen. Certificate of Completion Some programs issue a Certificate of Completion to the applicant. This typically includes: Household name and address Date of completion Description of work performed Organization name Signature of PM or caseworker This is a nice touch for the applicant and useful for grant documentation. Step 3: Upload Final After-Photos After-photos should: Match before-photo angles — same position, same lighting if possible Show the full repair area — wide shot plus close-up Be clearly labeled — "After - Roof repair, east section" Be uploaded to the case — not just stored on someone\'s phone Grant reporting tip: Funders love before-and-after comparisons. Good photos are the difference between "they said they did the work" and "here\'s the proof." Step 4: Reconcile Finances The Bursar should: Review all invoices — subcontractor bills, material receipts Compare to budget — did the project stay within the allocated amount? Process final payments — any outstanding invoices get paid Record expenses — all costs logged against the case Generate a financial summary — budgeted vs. actual, by category If the project went over budget: Document why (change order? under-estimate? unexpected issue?) Get admin approval for the overage Note the reason in the case file If the project came in under budget: Document the savings The excess returns to the organization\'s funding pool Financial accuracy matters. Grant auditors will look at individual case costs. Every dollar needs a receipt and a reason. Step 5: Close the Case in the System When everything above is done: Open the case in your dashboard Verify all projects are marked Complete — a case can have multiple projects; all must be done Set case status to "Concluded" — this signals the case is fully closed Log the closing event — "Case concluded on [date] — all repairs completed, applicant satisfied" Confirm the application status — if ALL repair needs across ALL cases are met, the Application status should also update to "Concluded" Step 6: Post-Completion Follow-Up 30 days after closure: Contact the applicant to confirm repairs are holding up Note any issues — roof still leaking? Plumbing working? If problems are found, the case can be reopened (see below) 90 days after closure: Final check-in (programs vary on whether this is required) Close the loop with the funder if they require post-completion reporting When Cases Get Reopened Sometimes a case needs to come back to life. Common reasons: Reason What Happens Repair failure — the roof leaks again Reopen case, assign corrective task, document the failure New damage — a storm causes additional damage This may be a NEW application, not a reopen Grant audit finding — funder questions costs Reopen case, gather documentation, respond to funder Applicant complaint — something wasn\'t done right Reopen case, assess the complaint, fix if warranted Reopening a case: Change status from "Concluded" back to "Active" Log the reopening reason Assign corrective tasks Set a timeline for resolution Close again when resolved Archiving Case Documents Once a case is concluded and the follow-up period has passed: All documents should be properly filed in the case record Photos should be organized (before/after by repair type) Financial records should be complete (receipts, invoices, payments) Communications should be logged (all interactions in the system) Retention periods vary by funder and program. Common requirements: Financial records: 7 years Photos: permanent Contracts: 10 years General case files: 5 years Quick Reference Action Where Who Final inspection On-site PM, Crew Lead Applicant sign-off On-site or phone Caseworker Upload after-photos Case detail → Documents Anyone Reconcile finances Case detail → Financial tab Bursar Close case Case detail → Status → "Concluded" PM, Admin Reopen case Case detail → Status → "Active" PM, Admin Archive documents Case detail → Documents Caseworker Remember: A closed case is a story with an ending. Make it a good one — the applicant, the funder, and your future self will all thank you.',
  ),
  28 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'conducting-home-assessment',
    'title' => 'Conducting a Home Assessment SOP',
    'category' => 'Standard Operating Procedures',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'assessment',
      1 => 'field-work',
      2 => 'photos',
      3 => 'estimates',
      4 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Conducting a Home Assessment Who this is for: Assessors who visit properties to evaluate repair needs and create estimates. Also useful for Caseworkers who coordinate assessments. ',
    'text' => 'Conducting a Home Assessment Who this is for: Assessors who visit properties to evaluate repair needs and create estimates. Also useful for Caseworkers who coordinate assessments. Why This Matters Quality assessments determine accurate estimates and successful repairs. A good assessment means the right crew shows up with the right materials. A bad assessment means they show up, realize the problem is twice as big as expected, and leave — and the applicant waits longer. Your assessment is the blueprint for everything that follows. Get it right and the whole project flows. Get it wrong and everyone pays for it — in time, money, and frustration. Before You Go: Preparation Checklist [ ] Review the application — what repair needs did the applicant report? [ ] Check eligibility flags — any red or yellow flags you should know about? [ ] Confirm the address — placecode and property address should match [ ] Contact the applicant — schedule a time that works for them [ ] Charge your phone/tablet — you\'ll be taking photos and filling out the assessment form [ ] Bring measuring tools — tape measure, level, moisture meter if available [ ] Bring safety gear — hard hat, flashlight, gloves if entering damaged areas Pro tip: Ask the applicant to gather any insurance documents, FEMA correspondence, or contractor estimates they may have. This saves a follow-up trip. On-Site: Step-by-Step Assessment 1. Start with the Exterior Walk the property perimeter first. Look for: Roof damage (missing shingles, sagging, visible holes) Siding damage (missing panels, water stains, warping) Foundation issues (cracks, settling, water intrusion) Gutter and drainage problems Tree damage affecting the structure 2. Assess Each Room Systematically For each room, check: Walls — water damage, mold, structural cracks Floors — warping, soft spots, water damage Ceiling — stains, sagging, active leaks Windows/doors — operable, sealed, damaged Electrical — outlets working, visible wiring damage Plumbing — water pressure, visible leaks, water heater condition 3. Document with Photos The photo rule: Every repair need gets at least two photos — one wide shot showing context, one close-up showing the specific issue. Photo best practices: Turn on location services — photos with GPS data are far more useful Include a reference object (ruler, hand, coin) for scale Take "before" photos from the same angle you\'ll use for "after" Label photos in the system with room + issue (e.g., "Kitchen - ceiling water damage") Important: Photos aren\'t just documentation. They\'re evidence for grant reporting, insurance claims, and proving the work was done. A case without photos is a case that can\'t be closed properly. 4. Fill Out the Assessment Form In the platform (mobile app or desktop): Open the assigned case Navigate to the Assessment section Select each repair category that applies (roof, plumbing, electrical, etc.) Describe the damage — be specific. "Roof leaking" is not as useful as "3\' × 4\' section of missing shingles on east-facing slope, active water intrusion into attic" Estimate severity — minor, moderate, severe, or urgent Note any safety hazards — structural instability, exposed wiring, mold 5. Create a Preliminary Estimate For each repair need, note: What trade(s) are required — roofing, plumbing, electrical, carpentry, etc. Rough scope — how much work? (e.g., "Replace 40 sq ft of subfloor in bathroom") Materials needed — approximate types and quantities Estimated labor hours — your best guess based on experience You don\'t need exact numbers here. A rough estimate is fine — the contractor or crew lead will refine it. But "I don\'t know" doesn\'t help anyone. Use your professional judgment. What If the Applicant Disagrees? Sometimes an applicant thinks their repair need is bigger (or smaller) than your assessment says. This is normal. How to handle it: Listen — the applicant knows their home better than you do Explain your findings — walk them through what you observed Document the disagreement — note it in the assessment. "Applicant reports additional leaking in basement not visible during assessment — recommend follow-up inspection" Don\'t argue — if there\'s uncertainty, flag it for the caseworker or PM to investigate Remember: The applicant has been through a disaster. They\'re stressed. Patience goes a long way. After the Assessment Submit the assessment in the platform Log a progress event — "Assessment completed by [Your Name]" Notify the caseworker — they should follow up with the applicant to discuss findings Upload any additional photos you didn\'t capture in the assessment form Quick Reference: Assessment Categories Category What to Look For Typical Trades Needed Roof Missing shingles, leaks, structural damage Roofer Plumbing Leaks, no water, damaged pipes Plumber Electrical Exposed wiring, non-functional outlets, panel damage Electrician HVAC No heat/AC, damaged ductwork HVAC technician Structural Foundation cracks, wall bowing, floor sagging General contractor Interior Water damage, mold, damaged drywall Carpenter, painter Accessibility Ramps, door widths, bathroom modifications General contractor Safety Exposed hazards, entry security Various Remember: Your assessment is the foundation of the repair plan. The better you document, the smoother the project goes.',
  ),
  29 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'document-management',
    'title' => 'Document Management — Uploading, Organizing & Finding Files',
    'category' => 'Standard Operating Procedures',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'documents',
      1 => 'uploads',
      2 => 'file-management',
      3 => 'photos',
      4 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Document Management: Uploading, Organizing &amp; Finding Files Who this is for: Everyone who uploads, views, or manages documents in the platform — which is every role. Why This ',
    'text' => 'Document Management: Uploading, Organizing &amp; Finding Files Who this is for: Everyone who uploads, views, or manages documents in the platform — which is every role. Why This Is Needed The document system is powerful but complex. Photos prove the work was done. Income docs prove eligibility. Contracts prove agreement. Every document in a case is evidence — for the applicant, the organization, and the funder. A case with no documents is a case that can\'t be closed properly. A case with disorganized documents is a case that fails an audit. What Kinds of Documents Exist Document Type Who Uploads When Why Income verification Applicant, Envoy During intake Proves eligibility Photo ID Applicant, Envoy During intake Confirms identity Before photos Assessor, Crew At assessment Documents damage During photos Crew, Volunteer During repair Shows progress After photos Crew, PM At completion Proves work done Contracts PM, Admin Before work starts Legal agreement Invoices Subcontractor, Bursar After work completed Payment request Assessments Assessor After site visit Repair scope Communication logs Anyone After any contact Record of interaction Progress notes Anyone As work happens Timeline evidence Uploading Documents Step-by-Step Open the case you\'re uploading to Navigate to the Documents section Click "Upload Document" Select the document type from the dropdown (income verification, before photo, etc.) Choose the file from your device Accepted formats: PDF, JPG, PNG Max file size: 25MB per file Add a description (optional but helpful) — "Before - Roof, east section" Upload Tip: Name your files before uploading. "before_roof_east.jpg" is infinitely more useful than "IMG_4721.jpg." Uploading Multiple Files at Once The platform supports multi-file upload. Select all the files you want to upload at once, and the system will process them as a batch. Best practice: Upload files of the same type together. Don\'t mix before photos with invoices — upload them as separate batches with appropriate document types. Photo Best Practices Before Photos Wide shot showing the full repair area Close-up showing the specific damage Include a reference for scale (hand, ruler, coin) Same angles you\'ll use for after photos Label with room/area — "Kitchen ceiling, northwest corner" After Photos Same angles as before photos (side-by-side comparison is powerful) Well-lit — turn on lights, open blinds, use flash Clean workspace — no tools, debris, or ladders in the shot Label clearly — "After - Roof repair, east section, 9/15/2026" During Photos Progress milestones — demo complete, rough-in installed, drywall hung Hidden work — things that will be covered up (wiring behind walls, subfloor under finish floor) Unexpected findings — "Opened wall, found mold on insulation" Grant reporting gold: Before/after side-by-side comparisons are the single most compelling evidence for funders. Take the time to get them right. Finding Documents In a Case Open the case and go to the Documents tab. You can: Filter by type — show only photos, only invoices, etc. Sort by date — newest first, or oldest first Search by description — if someone labeled files well Across Cases Use Case Search with document filters to find: All cases with missing after photos All cases with uploaded contracts All cases from a specific date range with documents Document Security Who Can See What Document Type Visible To Income verification Assigned caseworker, Admin, Bursar Photo ID Assigned caseworker, Admin Photos (before/during/after) All assigned staff, applicant Contracts PM, Admin, applicant Invoices Bursar, PM, Admin Assessments All assigned staff Sensitive documents (income verification, ID) have restricted access. Only people who need them for eligibility determination can view them. What Applicants Can See Applicants can see: Photos of their own property Contracts they\'ve signed Communication logs addressed to them Applicants cannot see: Other applicants\' documents Internal staff notes Financial records (invoices, payment records) Common Problems &amp; Solutions "My Upload Keeps Failing" Check file size — is it over 25MB? Compress it or split it Check file type — is it a PDF, JPG, or PNG? Other formats aren\'t accepted Check your connection — a slow internet connection can timeout large uploads Try a different browser — some browsers handle large uploads better "A Photo Disappeared" Check the trash/version history — deleted files may be recoverable Check if someone re-categorized it — it may be under a different document type Check the activity log — who uploaded it, and was it later deleted? "I Can\'t See a Document I Know Exists" Check your permissions — some documents are restricted to certain roles Check the case — are you looking at the right case? Ask the caseworker — they can see what documents are attached and who can view them Document Retention Different document types have different retention requirements: Document Type Retention Period Financial records (invoices, receipts) 7 years Contracts 10 years Photos Permanent Income verification 7 years Photo ID 3 years Assessments 5 years The platform retains documents according to these schedules. Don\'t manually delete documents — the system handles retention automatically. Quick Reference Action Where Who Upload document Case detail → Documents → "Upload" Everyone View documents Case detail → Documents tab Role-dependent Filter by type Documents tab → Filter dropdown Everyone Download document Document detail → "Download" Role-dependent Delete document Document detail → "Delete" (restricted) Admin only View activity log Case detail → Activity Log Everyone Remember: A document in the system is evidence. A document on someone\'s phone is a memory. Put it in the system.',
  ),
  30 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'editing-applicant-contact-info',
    'title' => 'Editing Applicant Contact Info',
    'category' => 'Standard Operating Procedures',
    'tags' => 
    array (
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Editing Applicant Contact Info When this comes up The contact details on a case sometimes belong to the wrong person — most often a referrer or neighbor who filled out the intake',
    'text' => 'Editing Applicant Contact Info When this comes up The contact details on a case sometimes belong to the wrong person — most often a referrer or neighbor who filled out the intake form on behalf of the person actually needing help. You usually find out through a communication log (e.g. a caseworker called the number and reached the referrer, not the applicant). The rule: never edit the raw submission Submissions (intake_submissions) are the preserved, as-received form data. Corrections belong on the application — the editable working copy created from the submission for intake and eligibility work. Cases hang off the application, so a correction on the application is reflected on every case for that household. submission (raw, never edited) -&gt; application (edit here) -&gt; case(s) -&gt; project(s) How to make the correction Open the case from the Case List (filterable by organization) and click through to Case Review. Under Applicant, click Edit contact info. Update first name, last name, email, and/or phone, then Save. What happens behind the scenes (implemented in the Vue demo and mirrored in app/): If an application already exists for the submission, only its applicant_first_name / applicant_last_name / applicant_email / applicant_phone fields are updated. If none exists yet, one is created as a copy of the submission first (same behavior as deduplicate_submissions in sql/application-lifecycle.sql), then your edit is applied. The raw submission row is left untouched either way. The Case Review shows an edited flag once an application backs the contact fields, so staff can tell corrected data from raw intake data. Follow-ups If the phone number on file belongs to a referrer, add a communication log entry noting whose number it was and how the correct one was obtained. If the address is wrong (not just contact info), that\'s a bigger change — the placecode and deduplication are keyed off the address; escalate to an org admin rather than editing in place.',
  ),
  31 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'home-repair-financial-mgmt',
    'title' => 'Financial Management — Budgets, Invoices & Payments SOP',
    'category' => 'Standard Operating Procedures',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'finance',
      1 => 'budget',
      2 => 'invoices',
      3 => 'grants',
      4 => 'audit',
      5 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Financial Management: Budgets, Invoices &amp; Payments Who this is for: Bursars managing case finances, Admins overseeing budgets, and Project Managers tracking project costs. Why ',
    'text' => 'Financial Management: Budgets, Invoices &amp; Payments Who this is for: Bursars managing case finances, Admins overseeing budgets, and Project Managers tracking project costs. Why This Is Crucial Grant compliance requires meticulous financial tracking. Errors here can cost funding — and when a funder pulls out, it doesn\'t just affect one case. It affects every household waiting for help. Money is the fuel. Without it, nothing moves. With it mismanaged, everything crashes. Understanding Case Budgets Where the Money Comes From Before any repair work starts, funding is secured from: Grants — Cisco, Land of Sky, Dogwood Trust, City Funds, VOAD partners Donations — individual, corporate, church groups In-kind contributions — donated materials, volunteer hours (these count!) Insurance payouts — when the household has applicable coverage The money flows like this: Funder → Organization Account → Project Budget → Subcontractor/Vendor Payment Each step needs documentation. No step can be skipped. Case Budget Structure Each project within a case has: Budgeted amount — what was approved for this scope of work Expensed amount — what\'s actually been spent Remaining balance — budget minus expenses Rule: Expenses cannot exceed the budget without a documented and approved change order. If a project runs over, someone needs to approve where the extra money comes from. Processing Subcontractor Invoices Step-by-Step Receive the invoice — from the subcontractor, for work completed Match to tasks — which repair tasks does this invoice cover? Verify completion — are those tasks marked Complete in the system with photos? Check the amount — does it match the quoted/contracted amount? Log the expense — enter the invoice amount against the project budget Upload the invoice — attach to the case document record Submit for approval — PM or Admin approves the payment Process payment — bursar issues payment per organizational process Record the payment — note payment date, amount, method in the system Invoice Red Flags Red Flag What It Means Action Amount exceeds quote Sub billed more than agreed Contact sub for explanation before approving No supporting documentation No receipts, no timesheets Request documentation before processing Tasks not marked complete Billing for unfinished work Verify completion before paying Duplicate invoice Same work billed twice Check case history for prior payments Unapproved change work Work outside the scope Requires change order approval first Tracking Expenses Against Grant Restrictions Grant Money Comes with Rules Different grants have different restrictions: Capping per household — e.g., maximum $50,000 per household Eligible expenses — some funders only cover materials, not labor Geographic limits — money can only be spent in certain counties Time limits — funds must be spent by a certain date Matching requirements — organization must contribute a percentage The bursar\'s job: Make sure every expense complies with all applicable restrictions. How to Track Tag each expense with the funding source it draws from Monitor caps — track total spent per household against the cap Check eligibility — is this expense covered by the tagged funder? Watch deadlines — are funds expiring soon? Calculate match — are we meeting matching requirements? When in doubt, over-document. An expense with too much documentation is never a problem. An expense with too little is an audit finding. Handling Denied or Partial Payments When a Subcontractor\'s Invoice Is Denied Document the reason — why was it denied? (incomplete work, over-billing, missing docs) Notify the subcontractor — explain what\'s needed to resolve Log the denial — in the case financial record, note the denied invoice and reason Track the resolution — when the sub provides what\'s needed, re-process When Only Partial Payment Is Approved Pay the approved amount — process the portion that\'s valid Document the reduction — note why the full amount wasn\'t approved Notify the subcontractor — explain the partial payment and what\'s needed for the remainder Keep the invoice open — until fully resolved Generating Financial Reports For Grant Reporting Funders typically need: Total spent — how much of their grant has been used Cases served — how many households benefited Average cost per case — total spent ÷ number of cases Expense breakdown — by category (materials, labor, admin) Geographic distribution — where was the money spent? Timeline — when was money spent? The platform should generate these reports from case data. If it doesn\'t, the bursar compiles them manually from the financial records. For Internal Use Budget vs. actual — per project and per organization Outstanding payables — what invoices are waiting for payment? Funding availability — how much money is still available to spend? Volunteer hour value — volunteer hours × reasonable hourly rate = in-kind contribution The Audit Trail: What Gets Logged and Why Every Financial Action Is Logged Action What\'s Logged Why Invoice created Amount, vendor, case, date Proof of obligation Invoice approved Approver, date, any notes Chain of authorization Payment made Amount, date, method, reference Proof of payment Budget adjusted Old amount, new amount, reason, approver Change documentation Expense denied Reason, denied by, date Decision record Refund issued Amount, reason, recipient Reversal documentation The immutable activity log records all of this automatically. You can\'t delete a financial entry — you can only add a correcting entry. This is by design. What Auditors Look For Matching amounts — invoice = approved amount = payment amount Supporting docs — every expense has a receipt or invoice Proper authorization — someone approved each payment Timely recording — expenses logged when they occurred, not added later Budget compliance — no over-spending without documented approval Audit tip: If an auditor asks "why did you do it this way?" the answer "because that\'s how the system works" is much stronger when the system has been doing it right from the start. Common Financial Scenarios Scenario 1: Project Goes Over Budget The assessment estimated $3,000 for roof repair. The actual cost is $4,500 because the damage was worse than expected. Process: PM creates a change order documenting the additional cost Admin approves the change order (confirming funding is available) Project budget is increased Additional funding source is tagged if needed Expense is logged against the new budget Scenario 2: Two Funders, One Case A household qualifies for both Cisco funding and a local church grant. Process: Tag expenses to specific funders (e.g., materials from Cisco, labor from church) Track each funder\'s cap separately Report to each funder only on their portion Ensure no double-counting of expenses Scenario 3: Volunteer Labor as In-Kind Contribution A volunteer team spends 40 hours on a case. Their labor has value. Process: Log volunteer hours against the case tasks Apply a reasonable hourly rate (e.g., $25/hr for general labor) Record the in-kind value ($1,000) in the case financial record Report to funders as in-kind contribution (many funders count this toward matching requirements) Quick Reference Action Where Who View case budget Case detail → Financial tab Everyone Log expense Case detail → "Add Expense" Bursar, PM Upload invoice Case detail → Documents Anyone Approve payment Case detail → Financial → "Approve" PM, Admin Process payment Financial module → "Pay Invoice" Bursar Generate report Reports → Financial Bursar, Admin Adjust budget Project detail → "Change Order" PM, Admin View audit trail Case detail → Activity Log Everyone Remember: Every dollar spent is a dollar donated by someone who believes in this work. Treat it like the trust it represents.',
  ),
