    'excerpt' => 'Managing Repair Projects Who this is for: Project Managers who oversee repair work and Crew Leads who manage on-site operations. Why This Matters This is the operational core of th',
    'text' => 'Managing Repair Projects Who this is for: Project Managers who oversee repair work and Crew Leads who manage on-site operations. Why This Matters This is the operational core of the system — turning assessments into completed work. Everything before this point is preparation. Everything after is documentation. This is where the actual repair happens. A well-managed project means the applicant gets their home fixed, the crew does good work, and the funder sees results. A poorly managed project means delays, budget overruns, and a household stuck in limbo. Step 1: Create Work Orders from the Assessment Once the assessment is submitted and approved: Open the case in your dashboard Review the assessment — what repairs are needed? Create a Project — give it a name (e.g., "42-Cherry: Roof &amp; Plumbing Repair") Break repairs into Repair Tasks — each task is one actionable item Task structure: Task name — specific and actionable ("Replace damaged shingles on east roof section") Assigned trade — roofing, plumbing, electrical, etc. Assigned to — crew member, subcontractor, or volunteer team Estimated hours — from the assessment Materials needed — list what\'s required Priority — urgent, normal, or low Rule of thumb: If two different people could work on it independently, it should be two separate tasks. "Fix the bathroom" is a project. "Replace subfloor," "Install new vanity," and "Repair shower surround" are tasks. Step 2: Assign Tasks To Crew Members Assign tasks to your in-house crew when: The work is within their skill set You have availability in their schedule The task doesn\'t require specialized licensing (electrical, plumbing may need licensed subs) To Subcontractors Assign to subcontractors when: The work requires a licensed professional The task is outside your crew\'s capabilities You need specialized equipment To Volunteers Assign to volunteers when: The task is volunteer-friendly (demo, painting, cleanup) Supervision is available (crew lead on-site) Safety risks are minimal Important: Volunteers should never be assigned tasks requiring licensed work, working at heights, or handling hazardous materials. Step 3: Material Requests If tasks require materials: Create a material request in the platform List items needed — be specific (not "lumber" but "2×4×8 pressure-treated, 12 pieces") Submit for approval — PM or admin approves the request Track delivery — note when materials arrive on-site Best practice: Order materials before the crew starts that task. Nothing kills momentum like "we can\'t do the work because the materials haven\'t arrived." Step 4: Track Progress on the Dashboard Your dashboard should show: Tasks by status — pending, in progress, complete, blocked Tasks by assignee — who\'s working on what Budget status — how much has been spent vs. allocated Timeline — start date, estimated completion, actual progress Update tasks daily: Mark tasks In Progress when work begins Mark tasks Complete when finished Add photos of completed work Log hours worked — this matters for grant reporting and volunteer recognition If you didn\'t log it, it didn\'t happen. Funders need to see hours, costs, and outcomes. Your dashboard data becomes their report. Step 5: Handle Change Orders Sometimes the scope changes. The crew opens a wall and finds mold. The assessment missed something. The applicant has an additional need. Change order process: Document the change — what\'s different from the original assessment? Estimate the impact — additional cost, time, materials Get approval — PM or admin approves the change Update the project — add new tasks, adjust budget, revise timeline Notify the applicant — they should know what changed and why Never do unapproved extra work. If it\'s not in the project scope and hasn\'t been approved through a change order, it doesn\'t get done. This protects the organization, the funder, and the applicant. Step 6: Coordinate Multiple Trades When multiple trades work on the same property: Sequence matters — electricians before drywall, plumbers before flooring Communicate the schedule — everyone should know when they\'re on-site Designate a site lead — someone on-site to coordinate (usually the crew lead) Log handoffs — when one trade finishes and another begins, note it Common sequencing: Emergency/stabilization first Structural repairs Roof and exterior envelope Plumbing and electrical rough-in Insulation and drywall Finish work (painting, flooring, fixtures) Final inspection and cleanup Step 7: Quality Control Checkpoints At key milestones, verify the work: Checkpoint What to Verify After demo All damaged material removed, structural issues visible After rough-in Plumbing/electrical/HVAC installed correctly before walls close After finish Work meets quality standards, no visible defects Final walkthrough Everything in scope is complete, site is clean, applicant is satisfied If something doesn\'t pass: Note the issue, assign a corrective task, and re-inspect when complete. Don\'t close a task that isn\'t actually done. Step 8: Mark Tasks Complete and Close Work Orders When all tasks in a project are complete: Verify each task — photos uploaded, hours logged, materials recorded Do a final walkthrough — with the applicant if possible Mark the project Complete in the platform Log the completion event — "Project [name] completed on [date]" Trigger the closeout process — invoice reconciliation, after photos, applicant sign-off Quick Reference Action Where Who Create project Case detail → "New Project" PM, Crew Lead Add tasks Project detail → "Add Task" PM, Crew Lead Assign tasks Task detail → "Assign To" PM, Crew Lead Request materials Project detail → "Material Request" Crew Lead Update task status Task detail → Status dropdown Anyone working on task Log hours Task detail → "Log Hours" Crew, Volunteers Add photos Task detail → "Upload Photos" Anyone on-site Approve change order Project detail → "Change Order" PM, Admin Close project Project detail → "Mark Complete" PM Remember: A project well-managed is a home well-repaired. The applicant is counting on you.',
  ),
