    'excerpt' => 'Decision 1 — What\'s the default landing view per role? The question: When an Admin / PM / Crew / Volunteer logs into Archer, what\'s the first thing they see, and in what layout? ',
    'text' => 'Decision 1 — What\'s the default landing view per role? The question: When an Admin / PM / Crew / Volunteer logs into Archer, what\'s the first thing they see, and in what layout? What Softr offers for collection views: List (with sub-layouts: cards, small cards, timeline, inbox) Grid (image-forward cards) Table (spreadsheet, dense scanning) Kanban (column-grouped board, drag-to-update) Calendar (date-anchored) Map (location-anchored) Org chart (hierarchical, niche) Sub-layout call-outs: Inbox layout (Pro plan+) — left sidebar list, right pane shows the selected item\'s details. Strong fit for triage/queue work where users process items one at a time. Worth prototyping for the triage queue. Timeline (Business plan+) — vertical date-anchored list. Useful for activity history. Patrick\'s starting recommendation: Admin → Inbox layout for triage; Table view available as a secondary tab for bulk review PM → Kanban grouped by phase as primary; List as secondary Crew → List (cards) filtered to "my assigned work this week" Volunteer → List (cards) filtered to "available to claim" Need from Ben: Confirm role priorities, push back where the work pattern is different from what I\'m assuming. Role Priority Hierarchy (top 3) Role 1st 2nd 3rd Admin Confirm Eligibility Contact/Schedule Edit Triage Notes: Filter: Confirmed Eligible!=✅ List: sorted by Triage 9-1, Date1-9 List: sorted by Triage 9-1, Date1-9, last Contact:9-1 List: sorted by Triage 9-1, Date1-9 Automatic Triage will sort “New Applicant List” and will Project Manager Active Projects Upcoming Projects Past Projects Notes Filter: Projects Status = Active Cards: Filter: Confirmed Eligible=✅, Status!=Active|Closed Status=Closed Crew Add Notes, Photos, Timesheets, Mark Repair Tasks Complete Review Upcoming Projects Review Past Projects Notes Details: Mobile Friendly Cards: Mobile Friendly Volunteer See Upcoming Projects Review Past Projects Set Preferences Notes No PII, No Address until Signed In Scope of Work, Site Lead, Dates, Notes Set Liked&amp;Disliked Trades/Tasks, Emergency Contacts, Dietary Restrictions, etc Level 11: ARCHR SuperAdmin Primary Responsibilities: Oversee system and platform, monitor bug reporting, documentation, and live support as needed. Information Access: Everything Action Permissions: Everything ↓ Level 10: Admin (Full system access) Primary Responsibilities: System configuration, user management, org settings, eligibility rules Information Access: Full system access within organization Action Permissions: CRUD all records, manage users, configure rules ↓ Level 9: Project Manager (Oversees projects) Primary Responsibilities: Oversees multiple projects, budget tracking, timeline management Information Access: All projects within organization, budgets, timelines, subcontractor quotes Action Permissions: Approve estimates, manage budget, coordinate resources ↓ Level 8: Bursar (Financial - separate track) Primary Responsibilities: Financial management, payments, invoicing, funding tracking Information Access: Financial data, payments, invoices, funding sources Action Permissions: Process payments, create invoices, track expenses ↓ Level 7: Caseworker (Manages cases) Primary Responsibilities: Primary point of contact, manages, applicant communication, coordinates services Information Access: All cases assigned to their org, applicant contact info Action Permissions: Update case status, communicate with applicants, request additional information ↓ Level 6: Assessor (Technical evaluation) Primary Responsibilities: Conducts home visits, evaluates repair needs, creates estimates Information Access: Case files assigned to them, property details Action Permissions: View cases, add assessments, upload photos, create estimates ↓ Level 5: Crew Lead (On-site supervision) Primary Responsibilities: Manages on-site repair crew, schedules work, ensures quality Information Access: Active repair projects, crew assignments, materials list Action Permissions: Assign crew members, update work progress, request materials ↓ Level 4: Subcontractor (Specialized trade) Primary Responsibilities: External trade specialist (plumbing, electrical, etc.) Information Access: Specific repair categories assigned to them Action Permissions: View assigned repairs, submit quotes, report completion ↓ Level 3: Envoy (Community support) Primary Responsibilities: Community Liaison, outreach, applicant support Information Access: Basic case information, applicant contact information Action Permissions: View case status, add notes, assist applicants with forms Organizational and program specific, not really project oriented ↓ Level 2: Crew Member (Physical work) Primary Responsibilities: Performs actual repair work Information Access: Daily assignments, task lists, safety information, hour logging Action Permissions: View tasks, mark complete, report issues ↓ Level 1: Requestor (Application only) Primary Responsibilities: Submits initial intake application, uploads documents Information Access: Own submissions only Action Permissions: Create, vew own, edit until submitted ↓ Level 0: Volunteer (Limited assistance) Primary Responsibilities: Unpaid helpers, supervised by Crew Lead Information Access: Limited task assignments, safety information Action Permissions: View assigned tasks, log hours',
  ),
  37 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'volunteer-mgmt',
    'title' => 'Volunteer Management & Hour Tracking SOP',
    'category' => 'Standard Operating Procedures',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'volunteers',
      1 => 'hours',
      2 => 'scheduling',
      3 => 'recognition',
      4 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Volunteer Management &amp; Hour Tracking Who this is for: Volunteers who want to help, Crew Leads who manage volunteer teams, and Admins who track volunteer contributions. Why It\'s',
    'text' => 'Volunteer Management &amp; Hour Tracking Who this is for: Volunteers who want to help, Crew Leads who manage volunteer teams, and Admins who track volunteer contributions. Why It\'s Important Volunteers are the lifeblood of disaster recovery. They show up, work hard, and ask for nothing but the satisfaction of helping their neighbors. Make it easy for them. Volunteer hours also count as in-kind contributions for grant reporting. Tracked properly, they\'re worth real money to the coalition. Tracked poorly, they\'re invisible — and the coalition loses funding because of it. Signing Up as a Volunteer For New Volunteers Complete the volunteer application — this generates your Level 0 volunteer account Set your profile — availability, skills, experience level, any trade preferences Complete any required training — safety orientation, platform basics Get assigned to a Crew Lead — you\'ll report to someone experienced Volunteer vs. Crew Member: Volunteers are unpaid helpers supervised by a Crew Lead. Crew Members are paid staff. The platform treats them differently — volunteers see available tasks to claim; crew members see assigned tasks. Profile Setup In your volunteer profile, fill out: General availability — when can you work? (weekdays, weekends, both) Skills &amp; experience — what can you do? (demo, painting, roofing basics, etc.) Physical limitations — any restrictions we should know about? (heights, heavy lifting) Emergency contact — who do we call if something happens? Dietary restrictions — if meals are provided on-site Viewing and Claiming Tasks What You\'ll See Your volunteer dashboard shows: Available tasks — volunteer-friendly tasks that need help Upcoming projects — where and when you can show up Your logged hours — running total of your contributions Your certifications — any training or clearance you\'ve completed Claiming a Task Browse available tasks — filtered to your area and skill level Click "Claim" — this reserves a volunteer slot for that task Show up — at the scheduled time and location Check in with the Crew Lead — they\'ll confirm your assignment Don\'t claim a task you can\'t complete. If your plans change, release the slot so someone else can take it. Clocking In and Out On-Site Time Tracking When you arrive on-site: Open the platform on your phone or the site tablet Navigate to your assigned task Click "Clock In" — this starts tracking your hours Work the task — do what the Crew Lead assigned Click "Clock Out" when done — this stops the timer If you forget to clock in: Tell the Crew Lead — they can manually add your hours Do it the same day — memories fade fast GPS verification: Some programs require GPS check-in to confirm you were on-site. This isn\'t about surveillance — it\'s about verifying volunteer hours for grant reporting. Logging Hours Worked What Gets Recorded Each clock-in session records: Date and time — when you worked Duration — how long you worked Task worked on — which repair task Location — which property Crew Lead confirmation — they verify you were there Why Accurate Hours Matter Reason Why Grant reporting Funders count volunteer hours as in-kind contributions Volunteer recognition Milestones (50 hrs, 100 hrs, 500 hrs) get certificates Project tracking PMs need to know how much labor went into each task Tax documentation Some programs provide documentation for volunteer tax deductions Reporting Volunteer Hours (For Crew Leads) End of Day Review the day\'s volunteers — who showed up? Verify logged hours — do the clock-in/out times look right? Add any manual entries — volunteers who forgot to clock in Submit the daily report — this logs all hours for the day End of Week Review the week\'s totals — any anomalies? Recognize top contributors — a shout-out goes a long way Plan next week\'s volunteer needs — what tasks need more hands? Volunteer Recognition &amp; Certification Milestones The platform tracks volunteer hours and recognizes milestones: Milestone Recognition 10 hours Thank-you email 50 hours Certificate of appreciation 100 hours Recognition at coalition meeting 500 hours Special recognition event Converting from Volunteer to Crew Member Sometimes a volunteer wants to become paid staff: Express interest — tell your Crew Lead or Admin Complete the staff application — separate from volunteer application Your volunteer history follows you — all your logged hours stay on your record Your role changes — from Level 0 volunteer to Level 1 or 2 staff Safety Rules for Volunteers Volunteers should NEVER: Work at heights without proper fall protection Handle hazardous materials (asbestos, lead paint, mold) Perform licensed work (electrical, plumbing, gas) Operate heavy machinery Work alone on-site Always: Follow Crew Lead instructions Wear provided safety gear Stay in your assigned area Ask if you\'re unsure about something Report any injuries immediately Quick Reference Action Where Who Set availability Profile → "My Availability" Volunteer Browse tasks Volunteer dashboard → "Available Tasks" Volunteer Claim a task Task detail → "Claim" Volunteer Clock in/out Task detail → "Clock In/Out" Volunteer Verify hours Task detail → Crew Lead view Crew Lead Add manual hours Task detail → "Add Hours" Crew Lead View volunteer report Reports → Volunteer Hours Admin, Bursar Assign volunteers Project detail → "Assign Volunteers" Crew Lead Remember: Every volunteer hour is a gift. Log it well, recognize it generously, and never take it for granted.',
  ),
  38 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'start-here',
    'title' => 'Welcome to the Asheville Regional Coalition for Home Repair (ARCHR) Platform',
    'category' => 'Start Here',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'tasks',
      1 => 'logs',
      2 => 'events',
      3 => 'documentation',
      4 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'Welcome to the Asheville Regional Coalition for Home Repair\'s Help section. If you find yourself on here, you may be an employee, contractor, administrator, volunteer, or simply so',
    'text' => 'Welcome to the Asheville Regional Coalition for Home Repair\'s Help section. If you find yourself on here, you may be an employee, contractor, administrator, volunteer, or simply someone exploring in the future how this system works. On September 28, 2024, our region was devastated by Hurricane Helene. Homes were completely uprooted in an instant. Many, many trees fell. We experienced, especially in our valleys and riverbound areas, flooding of up to 20 feet. We were not prepared for the level of complete destruction, nor would there have been any way to prepare. In the aftermath, we saw neighbors organizing and meeting each other, like never before. And making sure that others were taken care of. The worst of a natural disaster brought out the best in people helping people. Our coalition will cover disaster-related home repairs for qualifying households. Up to 50,000 dollars in This generosity extended to the level of non-profit institutions getting funding and forming partnership to enable projects like this. Hurricane Helene was deemed a 10,000 year probability natural disaster and described in it\'s damages and devastation as \'biblical.\' We are seeing more and more of these extreme catastrophic natural disasters around the world. We wanted to take the system we built, and automate it with a platform. Some aspirations with this tool: Create an automated system for every day people and organizations to provide aid after a natural disaster. Have this tool be known about before a natural disaster comes to your region. Make the process of mutual care after devastation some level of fun and game-like. There is nothing fun about the devastation and loss after a disaster, or repairing damaged roofing and drywall. But we believe game-ifying helping our neighbors survive the aftermath of a storm can bring out the best in us. Our existing home repair coalition is made possible by donors and partners, such as Cisco, Land of Sky, Habitat for Humanity, Community Housing Coalition of Madison County, PODER Emma.... Here are some helpful support documents you might be interested in reading next: How does this platform work? ARCHR 101 - Dummy Proof Edition - An overview of how this system works, from repair application, to completed home repair. Who Qualifies? Eligibility Determination &amp; Assuming Eligibility till proven otherwise - Our process assumes eligibility until proven otherwise. Eligibility is based on income, home ownership, cause of damage, demographics, and geography. Metrics, Math, and Money - How we make this all possible - How donors, organizations, and everyday people who give a damn, make this all possible. Here\'s some more technical support items on how to get started: How do I make an account? - The short answer is, you don\'t. There is not an option to register an account because that is not how this works. If you are in need of a home repair, you complete an intake form, and an account will be made for you at the end to track the status of your application. If you are looking to volunteer with us, we have a form for that as well that will generate a volunteer account. If you are a representative, employee, or administrator with a participating organization, we will make your accounts for you. For the public, there is a demo account you can log onto with placeholder data, so you can explore how the platform works. Read more here This project is open-source - You can start one in your region before a disaster hits. - As we develop, we are building an open source repository. This project is copylefted under GNU - General Public License. This allows others access to the code and freedom to change and modify, as long as the project is being used for it\'s original intention. This repository is under construction, and not available publicly yet. But if you are interested in once it is available, you can sign up for our news list. Read more here What information do I need to provide in an intake for home repair assistance? - To confirm eligibility, and to process an intake, we need certain information from applicants. Our goal is to only require the bare minimum information, and to not require more than that. Your privacy is important, and sometimes the less information we have on a private household, the better. But we do need to keep track of who lives there, who owns the home, the demographics data, household income, and geography. We will not share your private household information with law enforcement without a warrant, or with data brokers and ad agencies. Everything you share as an applicant is Protected and Privileged Information (PPI) under the [Privacy Act of 1974?]. For Developers and Technicians: Anatomy of a Home Repair Platform - Here\'s the different parts and systems of how this works under the hood! Current Platforms and The Great Code Migration - ARCHR is built on several software as a service platforms. We are actively migrating to a shareable code-based solution built on HTML5, PHP 8.1, JavaScript, and PostgreSQL. Learn More Here Schema-Mapping and Database - Mapping the flow of data - This is how it works on the back-end.',
  ),
  39 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'if-it-happened-log-it',
    'title' => 'TLE_STANDARD_OPERATING_PROCEDURE_v1',
    'category' => 'Technical Guides',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'tasks',
      1 => 'logs',
      2 => 'events',
      3 => 'documentation',
      4 => 'SOP',
    ),
    'excerpt' => 'TASKS, LOGS &amp; EVENTS: STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE Why We Log Everything One rule: If it happened, log it. Every task, every client communication, every scheduling note, every ',
    'text' => 'TASKS, LOGS &amp; EVENTS: STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE Why We Log Everything One rule: If it happened, log it. Every task, every client communication, every scheduling note, every event needs to be in the system. This is how we: Track what\'s been done and what\'s left Avoid duplicate work Know who to contact about what Show funders our impact Protect ourselves with documentation If you didn\'t log it, it didn\'t happen. When to Log If you... Log it as... Need something done Task Talk to a client on phone/email/in-person Communication Schedule an assessment or site visit Event Attend or plan a community event Event Notice something that needs attention Task Complete a repair step Task (with update) Take photos of work Task (attach photos) How to Log (Two Clicks) Quick Add (Anywhere, Anytime) Look for the + button next to "Tasks, Logs &amp; Events" in the sidebar Click it → a small form opens Enter two things: Which case/project this is for (start typing the address or case number) What needs to be logged (a brief description) Click Save → you\'re done That\'s it. The system captures: Who you are (auto-filled) When you logged it (auto-stamped) What you entered (your description) What If I Don\'t Know the Case Number? Type the client\'s name or street address. The system will suggest matching cases. What If I Have No Internet? Write it down. On paper. In a notes app. On a napkin. Then paste it into a Quick Add when you\'re back online. What If I Have 12 Things to Log? Log them all. Quick Add is designed for bulk entry. Log one, click "Save &amp; Add Another," repeat. The Review Process (Peer Review) Why Review? Quick entries are drafts. They\'re not counted in metrics or assigned to anyone until a Task Rabbit reviews them. Who Is a Task Rabbit? A Task Rabbit is someone (usually office-based) who: Has time to sit at a computer Knows the data fields Can add missing details Approves entries so they become real tasks What Task Rabbits Do Open the "Unverified" queue (it has a badge showing how many are waiting) Review each quick entry Add required fields: Priority (1-5) Who should do this (role or person) Due date Subtasks (if needed) Approve or reject the entry What Happens After Approval The entry becomes a real task or log entry It appears in the appropriate queue It gets counted in metrics The assigned person is notified Quick Log vs. Formal Entry Feature Quick Log Formal Entry (After Review) Who can do it Anyone Task Rabbits Required fields Case + Description Priority, Assignee, Due Date, Subtasks Counted in metrics ❌ No ✅ Yes Shows in task lists ❌ Unverified queue only ✅ Yes Time to complete 30 seconds 2-5 minutes When to use In the field, on the go At a desk, with time Common Scenarios Scenario 1: You\'re on Site, No Service What you do: Take notes on your phone. "Roof: 3 missing shingles. Left side. Need ladder." When you get back: Open Quick Add, paste your notes, log it. Scenario 2: You Just Got Off the Phone What you do: Open Quick Add, select the case, type: "Call with Sarah. She confirmed assessment for Dec 18 at 2pm. She will call if she needs to reschedule." Why: Next person who sees the case knows Sarah was contacted. Scenario 3: You\'re at a Desk and Have Time What you do: Go to the "Unverified" queue. Look for entries without enough detail. Fill in the missing fields and approve them. Why: This keeps the system accurate and helps everyone. Scenario 4: You Have a Lot of Tasks What you do: Use Quick Add for each one. Click "Save &amp; Add Another" after each. Why: Bulk entry is faster than writing them all down somewhere else. Tips for Writing Good Entries DO: Good Why "Schedule roof inspection - needs estimate for shingle replacement" Clear, actionable "Call with Maria - she will upload documents by Friday" Specific, follow-up possible "Need electrical quote - breaker panel is overloaded" Identifies the issue DON\'T: Not Helpful Why Not "Fix stuff" What stuff? "Talked to client" Who? About what? "Follow up" When? With whom? Golden Rule: Write enough detail that someone else could take over without having to ask you what you meant. Important Reminders Rule Why Log it immediately Memory fades. Don\'t wait. Include the case Otherwise nobody knows where it goes. Be specific Vague logs cause confusion. Trust the process Quick Add → Review → Approved → Done. Someone\'s watching The ledger never forgets. Every log is permanent. One Last Thing "Did it happen?" If yes, log it. "Does it need to happen?" If yes, log it as a task. "Can I add it later?" Yes, but add it now so you don\'t forget. Your logs are the memory of the organization. Make them count. Quick Reference Card (Printable)',
  ),
  40 => 
  array (
    'slug' => 'intake-privacy',
    'title' => 'What Information Do I Need to Provide in an Intake?',
    'category' => 'Technical Guides',
    'tags' => 
    array (
      0 => 'privacy',
