    'text' => 'Outline 1. Why Open Source? Natural disasters happen everywhere Every community should have access to disaster recovery tools Collaboration makes software better Don\'t rebuild what we already built 2. The License: GNU GPL v3 Copyleft license You can use, modify, and share Must keep source code open Can\'t use for proprietary purposes Must preserve original intention 3. What\'s Included in the Repository Component Description Frontend HTML5, CSS, JavaScript (mobile-first) Backend PHP 8.1 with PostgreSQL Database Schema Complete, documented schema Document Templates Contracts, certificates, assessments Translation Files English, Spanish, Ukrainian, Arabic Documentation SOPs, user guides, technical docs 4. Before a Disaster Hits - Be Prepared Deploy the platform before disaster strikes Customize for your region (languages, maps, organizations) Train staff and volunteers Test with mock scenarios Have documentation printed and ready 5. Repository Status Currently under construction Not yet publicly available Sign up for our news list to be notified Expected public release: Q2 2026 6. How to Contribute Code contributions (pull requests) Documentation improvements Translations to more languages Bug reports and feature requests Testing in different environments 7. Setting Up for Your Region Fork the repository Customize: organizations, service area maps, languages Set up cloud storage (S3, Dropbox, etc.) Configure email and notifications Test with your team Deploy and train',
